Add a `laplace init` command, run from inside a package repo (a directory
containing one or more .stan files but no laplace.toml yet). It should:

1. Scan every .stan file in the directory using the existing signature
   extractor (parser::signatures::extract_signatures).
2. Generate a laplace.toml with `name` guessed from the directory name,
   `version = "0.1.0"` as a default, and `exports` pre-filled with every
   function that HAS an `@laplace` doc comment (functions without one are
   assumed private/internal and left out, but print a note listing them so
   the user can add them manually if that guess is wrong).
3. Write the file, don't overwrite if laplace.toml already exists (error
   instead), and print a short summary of what was included/excluded so the
   user can immediately open and adjust exports by hand.

This turns manifest-writing into "review and adjust a generated file"
rather than "write one from scratch" -- the doc-comment convention already
carries the signal needed to make a reasonable first guess.
